    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-combatting-foreign-bribery-act-2024-sch-1-sec-70-3",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "70 5A  Failing to prevent bribery of a foreign public official",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-legislation-amendment-combatting-foreign-bribery-act-2024#sch-1-sec-70-3"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-legislation-amendment-combatting-foreign-bribery-act-2024.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-legislation-amendment-combatting-foreign-bribery-act-2024.pdf, p.13",
      "values:originalText": "Offence\n(1) A person (the first person) commits an offence if:\n(a) the first person is a body corporate:\n(i) that is a constitutional corporation; or\n(ii) that is incorporated in a Territory; or\n(iii) that is taken to be registered in a Territory under\nsection 119A of the Corporations Act 2001; and\n(b) an associate of the first person:\n(i) commits an offence against section 70.2; or\n(ii) engages in conduct outside Australia that, if engaged in\nin Australia, would constitute an offence (the notional\noffence) against section 70.2; and\n(c) the associate does so for the profit or gain of the first person.\n(2) Absolute liability applies to:\n(a) paragraphs (1)(a) and (c); and\n(b) the circumstance in subparagraph (1)(b)(i) that the associate\ncommits an offence against section 70.2; and\n(c) the circumstance in subparagraph (1)(b)(ii) that the associate\nengages in conduct outside Australia that, if engaged in in\nAustralia, would constitute the notional offence.\n(3) To avoid doubt, the first person may be convicted of an offence\nagainst subsection (1) because of the commission by the associate\n\nSchedule 1  Amendments",
      "skos:definition": "Offence\n(1) A person (the first person) commits an offence if:\n(a) the first person is a body corporate:\n(i) that is a constitutional corporation; or\n(ii) that is incorporated in a Territory; or\n(iii) that is taken to be registered in a Territory under\nsection 119A of the Corporations Act 2001; and\n(b) an associate of the first person:\n(i) commits an offence against section 70.2; or\n(ii) engages in conduct outside Australia that, if engaged in\nin Australia, would constitute an offence (the notional\noffence) against section 70.2; and\n(c) the associate does so for the profit or gain of the first person.\n(2) Absolute liability applies to:\n(a) paragraphs (1)(a) and (c); and\n(b) the circumstance in subparagraph (1)(b)(i) that the associate\ncommits an offence against section 70.2; and\n(c) the circumstance in subparagraph (1)(b)(ii) that the associate\nengages in conduct outside Australia that, if engaged in in\nAustralia, would constitute the notional offence.\n(3) To avoid doubt, the first person may be convicted of an offence\nagainst subsection (1) because of the commission by the associate\n\nSchedule 1  Amendments",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-combatting-foreign-bribery-act-2024-sch-1-sec-70-3-ss-1"
        },
